@ title: Why ensembling the public detectors is hard
@ subtitle: redundant detectors + a metric that punishes false positives
@ source: learning/ensemble_work/05_detection_agreement.py

--- note
## The obvious idea that doesn't work
"Combine the top public detectors" sounds easy. We measured it and it fails — and understanding
*why* is more useful than a cheap gain. Two facts kill the naive ensemble.

--- note
### Fact 1 — the detectors are redundant
**[Data — measured]** pilkwang and canqiang agree on **72%** of detections and have **identical**
node recall (~0.99 on labelled cells). They largely find the *same* cells. There's little unique
signal to combine.
--- output
| metric | value |
|---|---|
| detection agreement (pilkwang ∩ canqiang) | 72% |
| node recall (each) | ~0.99 |
| union recall gain | +0.03 (tiny) |

--- note
### Fact 2 — a union floods false positives
**[Domain]** Taking the **union** of two detectors' points means every disagreement becomes an
extra detection. Recall is already ~0.99 (nothing to gain), so the union mostly adds **FP edges**
near GT tracks → edge-precision drops → the score falls. The metric punishes exactly this.
--- code
union = merge(pilkwang_points, canqiang_points)   # recall barely moves (already 0.99)
# but disagreements -> extra edges near GT -> precision DOWN -> score DOWN
--- shape
union: recall +0.0<br>precision **down**<br>→ naive ensemble loses

--- note
### What *would* work
**[Craft]** The only safe fusion is at the **heat-map** level: average two detectors' probability
maps *before* peak-finding, so agreement reinforces and lone spurious peaks get suppressed — instead
of unioning final points. But given the redundancy, the real gains are elsewhere: **divisions**
(rs04) and **recall via gap2** (rs05).
--- shape
point-union → FP flood<br>heat-map average → safe<br>but redundancy caps it

